BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Art Exhibit: Painting in Fauve
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260806T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260806T180000
UID:260715Efn8vavDX0iUXM0s-IRQkQ
DESCRIPTION:Date: FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE  7/15/26\n\n**AUGUST EXHIBIT: PAI
 NTING IN FAUVE**\n\nEXHIBIT DATES:  **July 27 – August 30\, 2026**\n\n*
 *RECEPTION: SATURDAY\, August 1\, 2026\, 3-6 pm: Join us to meet Willow La
 Land and Gen Zorich\;  Learn about their art and celebrate the show.  Be
  sure to take part in the Gallery’s free monthlong drawing for a beautif
 ul work by a Gallery artist.  Refreshments will be served.**\n\nSonoma wi
 ne country artist\, **Willow LaLand** presents **Painting in Fauve\,** a c
 ollection of oil and acrylic paintings focusing on our region’s vibrant 
 beauty through the artist’s Fauvist lens.  Known for her hyper-realisti
 c landscapes\, these works take an imaginative turn to the fantastical\, t
 apping into the wild Fauve vivid color palette.   Highlighting the show\
 , “Mt. Tamalpais” comes alive\, swathed in rich colors cascading over 
 its undulating slopes\, while rich foreground purple/blues heighten the mo
 untain’s grandeur.  “Fitch Mountain” similarly seems to sing with c
 olor as it overlooks a lush vineyard.  LaLand’s color lens takes us fro
 m palette to palate as she captures the luxuriant purple/reds of a fine re
 d wine in “Sommelier’s Dream.”  Reflecting on her creative process\
 , LaLand notes “I love the artistic freedom to play\, create depth in co
 lor\, and give a tangible realistic impression within a completely fantast
 ical expression.”\n\nA third-generation artist\, LaLand started her stud
 ies as a young girl and had a studio space at 10 years old.  She is inspi
 red by early American realists and uses traditional techniques to achieve 
 dramatic realism.  LaLand finds the Fauve color palette elicits a joyful 
 response to her themes of nature and wine.  Many of her collectors love b
 oth her styles and have a hard time\, though they vastly differ\, choosing
  between the two.  LaLand exhibits widely\, including internationally\, a
 nd participated in the Art Trails Program.  She was featured in American 
 Art Collector magazine in 2013 and on KRCB in 2015.\n\nMeet the artist: La
 Land will host the Gallery on August 2\, 2:30 – 6pm and August 16 & 29\,
  11am to 6 pm.\n\nThe Stairway Smallworks Showcase features **Gen Zorich
 ’s Summertime\,** highlighting the beauty of wine country and the warmth
  of long\, lazy summer days.  From Zorich’s background as a geologist a
 nd environmental scientist\, she imbues her paintings with a deep understa
 nding of the natural world. Focusing on flora\, pink waterlilies floating 
 on pastel pads in “Summer Color” engender a sense of sun-filled easy d
 ays.  These works would bring a feeling of summer contentment any time of
  year.  An award-winning artist\, Zorich has exhibited widely and has bee
 n a longtime participant in the Sonoma County Art Trails Program.\n\nMeet 
 the artist: Zorich will host the Gallery on August 2\, 11am – 2:30pm\, A
 ugust 4\, 11am to 6 pm and August 21 & 23\, 2:30 – 6pm.\n\nAlso join us 
 for **ART NITE HEALDSBURG**\, Thursday\, August 6\, 5 – 7 pm.  ART NITE
  Healdsburg\, held the 1st Thursday of Every Month\, is a community event 
 celebrating art and creativity with over 20 galleries participating. This 
 month’s special theme: Music and Mocktails.  ArtNiteHealdsburg.com.\n\n
 Upstairs Art Gallery\, 306 Center Street\, Healdsburg\, CA 95448. (707) 43
 1-4214.: OPEN DAILY: 11am – 6 pm.    **www.upstairsart gallery.net** 
 \n\nFor further information: Contact: Beverly Bird\, Press Contact\, at bv
 drio@sonic.net or 707-799-1490.
LOCATION:Upstairs Art Gallery
URL:http://www.upstairsartgallery.net
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Coming Home to Yourself A Self-Compassion Series with Horses
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260806T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260806T193000
UID:260713YnHw1Zeqt0epMbXiVmX9vQ
DESCRIPTION:Coming Home to Yourself\n\nA Self-Compassion Series with Horse
 s\n\nThursday Evenings Open House: July 9\, 2026Series: July 16 – August
  27\, 2026\n\nNo Session on July 30th\n\nTime: 6-7:30pm\n\nComing Home to 
 Yourself  will be held at the Private Vacation Pasture of Heart & Mind E
 quine\, located on the border of Marin and Sonoma Counties\, along the 101
  freeway corridor\, close to the EarthRise Center at 101 San Antonio Road.
  Directions sent after registration.\n\nCome explore our self-compassion s
 eries with horses\n\nIn this series participants will interact with the ho
 rses to build trust\, establish boundaries\, and mirror the animals' prese
 nt-moment awareness\, helping to heal trauma\, reduce anxiety\, and improv
 e self-care.\n\nYour Registration Includes:\n\n- Six guided equine-assiste
 d self-compassion experiences\n\n- Facilitated group discussions and refle
 ction\n\n- Hands-on interactions with the horses\n\n- Workbook and between
 -session practices\n\n- Sound healing and breathwork exercises\n\n- Journa
 ling and creative expression activities\n\n- A supportive community of fel
 low participants\n\n- Ongoing opportunities to deepen self-awareness\, sel
 f-trust\, and self-care\n\nNo horse experience required\n\nLearn more abou
 t Heart & Mind Equine here: https://www.heartmindequine.com/
LOCATION:Heart and Mind Equine
URL:https://www.alanefreund.com/self-compassion-with-horses/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Imaginists present: BEACHED
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260806T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260806T203000
UID:260625mLbY7Eg8q0-ENKQWaSymew
DESCRIPTION:A new play by G.B. Lindsay.\n\nOld friends\, Jaycoen \\(Stephe
 n K Patterson\\) and Ougein \\(GB Lindsay\\) wake up to another delightful
  day at the beach as ocean sings and war rages on. But Today enters like n
 o other Yesterday\, for this day has arrived with new twisted turns and bu
 rns\, tucked-up into its sun-worn sleeves. Enter six extraordinary beings 
 \\(mostly Taylor Diffenderfer\\) who will test the limits of their friends
 hip and the inevitability of their fate.\n\nBeached is an absurdist tragic
 omedy in the tradition of Laurel and Hardy\, Beckett\, Kafka\, Tom Stoppar
 d\, and Eric Overmyer.
LOCATION:The Imaginists
URL:https://theimaginists.org/beached-julyaug
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jack London's Medicine Case: The Drugs He Used in His Life and Lit
 erature
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260806T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260806T123000
UID:260608kkEr81jCBk-8DwQlvzFsyg
DESCRIPTION:Step inside the House of Happy Walls Museum for an exploratio
 n of Jack London’s personal medicine case and what it reveals about his 
 life\, health\, and writing. Dating from 1909–1910\, the black leather S
 quibb medicine case on display in the museum contains 22 glass vials with 
 metal screw caps and drugs mostly in tablet form. The drugs in the case in
 clude various opioid and non-opioid analgesics\, antipyretics \\(fever red
 ucers\\)\, anti-inflammatories\, drugs for respiratory disorders \\(bronch
 itis\, the flu\, and expectorants\\)\, diuretics\, antiseptics\, and drugs
  to treat rheumatism and malaria.\n\nThis engaging presentation will exami
 ne the medicines known to have been used by Jack London himself and explor
 e his understanding of germ theory\, the physiological actions of the drug
 s he used\, and their potential for toxicity. Visitors will also discover 
 how London incorporated these medicines into the dramatic fabric of his no
 vels and short stories\, including “The Sea-Wolf\,” “The Little Lad
 y of the Big House\,” “Adventure\,” “The Red One\,” and “The 
 Kanaka Surf.”\n\nThrough history\, literature\, and medical science\, th
 is special talk offers a unique window into the mind of one of America’s
  most influential writers and the role medicine played in both his daily l
 ife and creative imagination.\n\nAbout the speaker:\n\nRichard M. Rocco\, 
 PhD\, is Associate Professor of Pharmacology at Samuel Merritt University
 \, with current research interests focused on methods for detecting counte
 rfeit anti-malarial drugs\, as well as the history of medicine and drug di
 scovery.\n\nHis interest in Jack London began after a visit to the Hous
 e of Happy Walls Museum\, where the Squibb medicine case was on display. T
 hat discovery led to several published papers exploring Jack London’s li
 fe and his use of medicine in literature. This presentation will mark the 
 first major talk focused specifically on London’s use of this particular
  medicine case.\n\nThis is part of a quarterly speaker series\, co-present
 ed by the Jack London Society and Jack London Park Partners\, in honor of 
 Jack London’s 150th birthday year.  The series features a range of expe
 rts and scholars\, sharing insights into Jack London’s life\, work\, and
  legacy and his connections to literature\, photography\, and the Bay Area
 .
LOCATION:Jack London State Historic Park
URL:https://jacklondonpark.com/events/jl-speaker-series-richard-rocco/
X-SOURCE:Press Democrat
END:VEVENT
